    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>After twelve years of study and organising in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, Nkrumah returned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a> in 1947 and built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/convention-peoples-party">Convention People's Party</a>, whose landslide in 1951 brought him from prison to the head of government.</li><li>He led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a> to independence in 1957 and made it a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pan-africanism">Pan-Africanism</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/decolonisation">decolonisation</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, while spending heavily on roads, harbours, schools and the Akosombo Dam.</li><li>His rule grew authoritarian, with detention without trial, a press under state control and, from 1964, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a> with himself as president for life, until the military and police overthrew him in 1966.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>While studying at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>, Nkrumah worked with the linguist William Everett Welmers, providing the spoken material on which the first descriptive grammar of his native <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fante">Fante</a> dialect of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akan">Akan</a> language was based.</p></aside>
    </section>

